  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Promoting Durable Shelter and Livelihood Solutions for Returned Internally Displaced Persons in Adamawa State in North-East Nigeria</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project seeks to provide returned internally displaced persons (IDPs) in Adamawa State, Nigeria with sustainable shelter and livelihood opportunities, whilst building capacity of government entities to lead the process in a principled manner and host communities to support the reintegration of former IDPs in an environment that promotes peaceful coexistence and reduces reliance on humanitarian assistance. The International Organization for Migration (IOM) envisages that if social cohesion and individual and community resilience is strengthened, government leadership and capacity is improved, and IDPs, together with returned IDPs and host communities, have access to integrated services and sustainable livelihoods, then communities will become more self-reliant and better able to achieve durable solutions because they will have both the capacity and opportunities to support themselves and their families.</narrative>
  </description>
